# Kolabo edge, for EVERY environment rather than one named deployment.
#
# Caddy is here for one reason above all others: FedaPay will not talk to this
# deployment over plain HTTP. The hosted checkout redirects the payer back to
# PAYMENT_RETURN_URL and the gateway POSTs webhooks to /webhooks/fedapay, and
# both of those have to be public HTTPS URLs with a valid certificate. Caddy
# obtains and renews them from Let's Encrypt with no cron and no certbot.
#
# TWO HOSTNAMES, deliberately:
#   {$WEB_HOSTNAME}  the built SPA, served as static files
#   {$API_HOSTNAME}  FastAPI
#
# The split is what CORS_ORIGINS in the backend already assumes, and it means
# the API can move to its own box later without touching the web deploy.
#
# THE HOSTNAMES USED TO BE WRITTEN OUT HERE AS kolabo.mocktar.com AND
# api.kolabo.mocktar.com, and that single fact was the reason a second
# environment could not exist. This file is tracked in git and bind mounted
# read only into the caddy container, so the dev box's hostnames were compiled
# into the repository: standing up production meant either editing a tracked
# file on the server (which the next `git pull` reverts, silently, taking TLS
# with it) or maintaining a fork of the deploy directory. Both hostnames now
# come from deploy/.env, which is per box and gitignored, so one branch serves
# both environments and neither can be pulled out from under the other.
#
# AN UNSET VALUE IS A REFUSAL TO START, NOT A DEFAULT. Caddy substitutes the
# empty string and then cannot parse a site block with no address, so the
# container exits and says so. That is deliberate: the alternative to a loud
# failure here is a box that answers on the wrong name, or answers everything,
# and gets a certificate to match. bootstrap-env.sh lists both variables in the
# set-these-by-hand block for the same reason.

{
	# Let's Encrypt sends expiry warnings here. Set ACME_EMAIL in deploy/.env.
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}

	# WHICH CERTIFICATE AUTHORITY TO ASK, and a required choice rather than an
	# inherited one for the same reason the hostnames above are.
	#
	# LET'S ENCRYPT ALLOWS FIVE CERTIFICATES PER HOSTNAME PER WEEK AND COUNTS
	# FAILED CHALLENGES. So the expensive mistake is not a broken deploy, it is
	# a broken deploy repeated: a first boot whose DNS has not propagated yet,
	# or whose hostname carries a typo, can spend the week's allowance in an
	# afternoon and leave the real name unable to get a certificate until the
	# window rolls. There is no appeal and no override.
	#
	# SO THE FIRST BOOT OF A NEW BOX GOES AT THE STAGING ENDPOINT. Its limits
	# are far looser and it issues certificates signed by a CA no browser
	# trusts. That warning in the browser is the result you want on a dry run:
	# it means the stack came up, the challenge was answered and TLS
	# negotiated, and the only thing wrong is the signer. Then ACME_CA is moved
	# to the production endpoint and `bash up.sh` runs again.
	#
	# AFTER SWITCHING, CHECK WHAT IS ACTUALLY SERVED from somewhere other than
	# the box, with no -k and no --insecure, before putting the hostname in
	# front of anyone. A dry run that was never switched back looks completely
	# healthy from the server's own logs.
	#
	# Both URLs are written out in deploy/.env.example. An empty value is a
	# caddy that cannot parse this block, and checks/preflight-env.sh refuses
	# before that happens and names which endpoint is selected on every run, so
	# a box cannot sit on staging unnoticed.
	acme_ca {$ACME_CA}
}

{$WEB_HOSTNAME} {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy web:80

	header {
		# The SPA is same-origin only; the API is the sole cross-origin thing
		# it talks to and it is named explicitly rather than allowed by
		# wildcard.
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
	}
}

{$API_HOSTNAME} {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy backend:8000

	header {
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
	}

	# CORS is answered by FastAPI's middleware from CORS_ORIGINS, not here.
	# Two layers both setting Access-Control-Allow-Origin is how you end up
	# with duplicated headers that browsers reject outright.

	# The webhook endpoint is deliberately NOT special-cased. It needs no
	# allowlist at this layer: FedaPay does not publish stable egress IPs, and
	# the real gate is the HMAC signature, the 300-second replay window and the
	# idempotency key, see backend/app/modules/payments/webhooks.py. An IP
	# rule here would give a false sense of protection and break silently the
	# day they change ranges.
}

# ANYTHING ELSE THIS BOX FRONTS. One line, and it is the one that stops this
# file from being edited on a server ever again.
#
# THE DEV BOX WAS ALREADY DOING THAT, and nothing in git could show it. This
# container is the shared TLS edge for three products, not one: Kolabo's two
# hostnames plus FastaNow's three and Educazzi's three, eight in total, added
# straight into this tracked file on the box on at least three separate
# occasions (Caddyfile.before-fastanow, .before-fastanow-web, .before-educazzi
# were sitting beside it). So the repository's copy of this file was a fiction,
# `git pull` refused to run, and the first deploy that forced past that refusal
# would have taken two other products down with no warning and no obvious cause.
#
# Site blocks for anything that is not Kolabo now go in deploy/caddy-extra/,
# which is gitignored and therefore per box. Nothing about the other products
# enters this repository, and nothing this repository ships can delete their
# routing.
#
# WHAT THE IMPORTED BLOCKS CAN AND CANNOT REACH. Caddy resolves an upstream name
# when a request arrives, not when the config loads, so a block naming a
# container that is not on any network caddy has joined loads fine and answers
# 502. Joining that network is the other half, and it belongs in
# docker-compose.local.yml rather than here: see the .example beside it.
import /etc/caddy/extra/*.caddy
